{:edb/db-before-t 2432 :edb/db-after-t 2433 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535745 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:27:19.454Z" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221129 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221129 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - 2y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 4x(t) + y(t) - 6$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T$. What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535745 false] [17592186221129 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/2c994a3f48f4d9e12a179b03538977f72e033c5e539ec6f73c6d4529c1674299.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/d1aac4dbbbd1d7579a0a03c1440b3140efc18f43d6a8016e867e9419af693675.png)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/6118cf06e6accf9f29665d66c4c4d00e0e7e2eb8bdbd98bd9044ee418422ca59.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/fb0d2631e167027b68263b62af5ba65d91442fdc89d1c16e39d6299f97835307.png)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221129 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nu^{′} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u + \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{u^{′}(t) = 3u(t) - 2v(t); v^{′}(t) = 4u(t) + v(t)\n$$" 13194139535745 false] [17592186221130 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221130 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = x^{2}(t) + x(t) + y(t) + 2; y^{′}(t) = 5x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 3y(t) + 10$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T$. What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535745 false] [17592186221130 1145 "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/951509e719a40caba7ecc5872d983b6cf83128d77af670c88846d2a06d329d8e.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/a3d02714f52ce42ec3e6d78d9cdd91f4c2ca14a59948f078d93318c94ec5e31a.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/cce8b4b07839490b3be0841f8a4e6e73b69426426504625cedecf738947b046d.png)\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad\ny(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad\nx'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad\nv'(t) = y'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+(v(t)-2)+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =5u(t)-(v(t)-2)^{2}+3(v(t)-2)+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)+4)+3v(t)-6+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221130 1145 "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad y(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad x'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad v'(t) = y'(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n$x^{′}(t)|= x^{2}(t) + x(t) + y(t) + 2; u^{′}(t)|= u^{2}(t) + u(t) + (v(t) - 2) + 2; = u^{2}(t) + u(t) + v(t)$.\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n$y^{′}(t)|= 5x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 3y(t) + 10; v^{′}(t)|= 5u(t) - (v(t) - 2)^{2} + 3(v(t) - 2) + 10; = 5u(t) - (v^{2}(t) - 4v(t) + 4) + 3v(t) - 6 + 10; = 5u(t) - v^{2}(t) + 7v(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{u^{′}(t) = u^{2}(t) + u(t) + v(t); v^{′}(t) = 5u(t) - v^{2}(t) + 7v(t)\n$$" 13194139535745 false] [17592186221131 1100 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\n1. $y=3x+3$\n2. $y=-x$\n3. $y=3x$\n4. $y=-3x$" 13194139535745 false] [17592186221131 1100 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\n- $y=3x+3$\n\n- $y=-x$\n\n- $y=3x$\n\n- $y=-3x$" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221131 1145 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x$." 13194139535745 false] [17592186221131 1145 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x.$" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364487 13194139535745 true] [17592186221354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364494 13194139535745 true] [17592186221354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364501 13194139535745 true] [17592186221354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364508 13194139535745 true] [17592186221354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364515 13194139535745 true] [17592186221355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364522 13194139535745 true] [17592186221355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364529 13194139535745 true] [17592186221355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364536 13194139535745 true] [17592186221355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364543 13194139535745 true] [17592186221356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364480 13194139535745 true] [17592186221356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364550 13194139535745 true] [17592186221356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364557 13194139535745 true] [17592186221356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364564 13194139535745 true] [17592186221914 1017 #edb/ref 17592186220435 13194139535745 false] [17592186221914 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364474 13194139535745 true] [17592186221914 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535745 true] [17592186221914 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221914 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = x^{2}(t) - 2x(t) + y(t) - 1; y^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 4y(t) - 7$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1, 2]^{T}$. Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139535745 false] [17592186221914 1145 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}(t)-2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+2)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1-2u(t)-2+v(t)+2-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+4(v(t)+2)-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-{v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)-4+4v(t)+8-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221916 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535745 true] [17592186221916 1100 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $x^{∗}$. One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y = 2x - 3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u = x - x^{∗}$. Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" 13194139535745 false] [17592186221916 1100 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=2x-3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" 13194139535745 true] [17592186221916 1145 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=2x.$" 13194139535745 true] [17592186364474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364475 13194139535745 true] [17592186364474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364476 13194139535745 true] [17592186364474 1000 #edb/ref 1